<BaseLayout
  title="Shelter Cove to Loring"
  description="Linking the new Shelter Cove Road to Patching Lake Cabin, Heckman Lake Cabin, and the Naha River Trail to Loring — the first walk-in access to the Naha from Ketchikan's road system."
  canonical="/projects/naha-connector"
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <p class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-tide">Project</p>
    <h1 class="font-display mt-2 text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
      Shelter Cove to Loring: The Naha Connector
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink/90">
      Linking the new Shelter Cove Road to Patching Lake Cabin, Heckman Lake Cabin, and the
      Naha River Trail to Loring — the first walk-in access to the Naha from Ketchikan's
      road system.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-6 border border-muskeg/60 bg-muskeg/10 px-4 py-3 text-sm leading-relaxed">
      Values shown like <Pending>[this]</Pending> are placeholders that still need a local
      number — off GIS or a recon trip, not off a website. The divide mileages on Segments A
      and B are planning estimates, not surveyed lines.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-display mt-12 text-2xl font-semibold">What exists today, and what doesn't</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed">
      In October 2025 the State of Alaska finished a decade of work and opened Shelter Cove
      Road to the public — 7.29 miles of new gravel connecting Ketchikan's road system to the
      Forest Service road network at Carroll Inlet. DOT&amp;PF built it for a stated reason:
      road access to public lands around Ketchikan is limited, and a household survey found
      unmet demand for recreation and subsistence access on the surrounding lands.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed">
      Northwest of that new road lies the Naha — a chain of lakes running seventeen miles down
      to saltwater at Loring, holding two Forest Service cabins on Heckman Lake, one at Jordan
      Lake, one at Patching Lake, and some of the best steelhead water in Southeast Alaska.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg font-semibold">Nobody can walk there.</p>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed">
      Patching Lake Cabin, built in 1975, is floatplane-only, and in fall and winter it isn't
      reachable at all once the lake freezes and planes can't land — which is precisely deer
      season. Heckman Lake Cabin can be reached on foot only by first hiring a boat or plane to
      Naha Bay, 23 miles from Ketchikan, and hiking the 5.8-mile
      <a href="/network/naha-river" class="underline decoration-contour underline-offset-4 hover:text-tide">
        Naha River National Recreation Trail</a> inland. Southeast Heckman Cabin is
      floatplane-only. Jordan Lake Cabin isn't reachable by most floatplanes at all, so people
      land at Heckman and walk two miles.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed">
      Every one of these is public infrastructure, already built, already maintained with
      public money, and all of it is gated behind a charter fare.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-display mt-12 text-2xl font-semibold">What we propose</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed">
      Two new trail segments completing a continuous route from the Ketchikan road system to
      saltwater at Loring:
    </p>
    <div class="mt-6 grid gap-4">
      <div class="border border-flagging/70 bg-flagging/5 p-5">
        <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold">
          Segment A — Shelter Cove Road to Patching Lake Cabin
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed">
          <Pending>[X]</Pending> miles from
          <Pending>[SPECIFIC ROAD SPUR / MILEPOST]</Pending> over the divide into the upper
          Naha drainage, terminating at the cabin at the north end of Patching Lake.
          Conceptual alignment only — not surveyed.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="border border-flagging/70 bg-flagging/5 p-5">
        <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold">
          Segment B — Patching Lake to Heckman Lake
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed">
          <Pending>[X]</Pending> miles down the Naha corridor, connecting Patching Lake Cabin
          to Heckman Lake Cabin and tying into the existing Naha River National Recreation
          Trail. Conceptual alignment only — not surveyed.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="border border-contour/70 p-5">
        <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold">Existing — Naha River NRT</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed">
          5.8 miles from Heckman Lake to the floating dock at the head of Naha Bay, adjacent to
          Loring. Already built, boardwalk and native tread, in need of ongoing maintenance we
          would take on. <a href="/network/naha-river" class="underline decoration-contour underline-offset-4 hover:text-tide">See the trail page</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-6 leading-relaxed">
      Completed, the route runs road system → Patching → Heckman → Jordan → Roosevelt Lagoon →
      Naha Bay → Loring: four public cabins and a National Recreation Trail, on foot, for the
      price of gas.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-display mt-12 text-2xl font-semibold">Why a trail, and only a trail</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed">
      The Naha is one of twelve Land Use Designation II areas Congress created in the Tongass
      Timber Reform Act of 1990. LUD II areas are managed in a roadless state to retain their
      wildland character, and those boundaries are set by statute — the Forest Service cannot
      change them, and the current Forest Plan revision explicitly leaves them alone.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed">
      There will never be a road into the Naha. But LUD II was written to be less restrictive
      than wilderness specifically to accommodate recreation access. A trail is the only lawful
      way to reach this country without an aircraft, and it is exactly the kind of access the
      designation contemplates.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed font-semibold">
      This project is not choosing trail over road. Trail is the only thing there is.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-display mt-12 text-2xl font-semibold">Precedent</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed">
      In 1901 the Loring cannery built the Fortmann Hatchery on Heckman Lake and ran a tramway
      along the Naha River to connect it to tidewater. A constructed transportation corridor
      followed this alignment 125 years ago, in service of an industry that has since left.
      What we're proposing is a lighter-touch return to a route this valley has used before.
      <a href="/naha" class="underline decoration-contour underline-offset-4 hover:text-tide">More on the history of the Naha</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-display mt-12 text-2xl font-semibold">Who it serves</h2>
    <dl class="mt-4 grid gap-4">
      <div>
        <dt class="font-display text-lg font-semibold">Hunters</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 leading-relaxed">
          Foot access to the Naha drainage for Sitka blacktail deer, in country currently
          reachable only by air — and, at Patching, not reachable by air at all once the lake
          ices. A road-system trail is open in November when a floatplane isn't. Black bear and
          wolf are also present in the drainage.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="font-display text-lg font-semibold">Anglers</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 leading-relaxed">
          Walk-in access to steelhead water the Naha is regionally famous for — spring and fall
          runs — plus salmon July through September, and year-round Dolly Varden and cutthroat
          in the river, Jordan Lake, and Heckman Lake. Cutthroat in Patching and Chamberlain
          Lakes at the upper end.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="font-display text-lg font-semibold">Backcountry hikers and trail runners</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 leading-relaxed">
          A multi-day, cabin-to-cabin traverse from the road system to saltwater, crossing an
          entire watershed. Nothing like it currently exists out of Ketchikan.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="font-display text-lg font-semibold">The community of Loring</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 leading-relaxed">
          A land connection to a boat- and plane-access community for the first time in its
          history.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="font-display text-lg font-semibold">Subsistence users</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 leading-relaxed">
          The same access DOT&amp;PF cited as the purpose of the road that now ends at the edge
          of this country.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <h2 class="font-display mt-12 text-2xl font-semibold">Design considerations</h2>
    <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-3 pl-5 leading-relaxed">
      <li>
        <strong>LUD II compatibility.</strong> Primitive ROS class, minimal structure,
        hand-tool construction where practical, no motorized corridor. Design intent is a trail
        that reads as part of the landscape rather than an intrusion into it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Muskeg and drainage.</strong> <Pending>[X]</Pending> feet of the alignment
        crosses saturated ground requiring boardwalk or turnpike. In this rainfall regime, tread
        not designed for water braids within a season.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The divide crossing on Segment A.</strong>
        <Pending>[Elevation gain, aspect, snow retention, avalanche or slide exposure if any]</Pending>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Seasonal usability.</strong> Explicit goal of a route passable in the fall
        hunting season and shoulder months when floatplane access to the lakes is unreliable or
        gone.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Wildlife.</strong> Corridor design and sightlines accounting for bear activity
        along an anadromous river, especially July–September.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Existing trail maintenance.</strong> The Naha River NRT's boardwalk sections and
        the Roosevelt Lagoon portage need ongoing attention. A connector is worthless if the leg
        it feeds into has failed.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="font-display mt-12 text-2xl font-semibold">Known constraints, stated plainly</h2>
    <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-3 pl-5 leading-relaxed">
      <li>
        <strong>Federal environmental analysis governs the schedule.</strong> Any new trail
        construction in a LUD II area on National Forest System land requires Forest Service
        authorization and NEPA review. We do not control that timeline and would rather say so
        now.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Orton Ranch.</strong> The existing Naha River NRT crosses the private grounds of
        Orton Ranch, where the Forest Service asks users to be respectful of the grounds and
        residents. Long-term public access on this route depends on that relationship, and it
        needs to be addressed openly rather than assumed.
        <Pending>[Current status of any easement or understanding]</Pending>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The Roosevelt Lagoon tide race.</strong> The salt chuck at the lagoon outlet
        runs as whitewater on the tide. There's an existing portage and shelter; both need
        maintenance and clear signage.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Mileages across the divide are not yet surveyed.</strong> Segment A and B
        lengths in this document are planning estimates pending GIS work and ground recon.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Winter access to Patching.</strong> The lake freezes; that's the whole point of
        the trail, and it also means construction and resupply windows are narrow.
      </li>
    </ul>

